.TH FREAD 3
.SH NAME
fread, fwrite \- binary input and output
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <stdio.h>

int
fread(ptr, size, nitems, stream)
char *ptr;
unsigned size, nitems;
FILE *stream;

int
fwrite(ptr, size, nitems, stream)
char *ptr;
unsigned size, nitems;
FILE *stream;
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B fread
reads up to
.I nitems
items of
.I size
bytes each from
.I stream
into the memory at
.IR ptr .
.B fwrite
writes
.I nitems
items of
.I size
bytes each, from the memory at
.IR ptr ,
to
.IR stream .
The bytes go through the stream's buffer, unchanged.
.PP
To read or write a structure, or an array:
.PP
.nf
	struct rec r;
	long table[100];

	fread((char *)&r, sizeof r, 1, fp);
	fwrite((char *)table, sizeof table[0], 100, fp);
.fi
.SH "RETURN VALUES"
Each returns the number of whole items read or written.
.B fread
returns fewer than
.I nitems
at the end of the file or after an error;
.BR feof (3)
and
.BR ferror (3)
tell which.
.B fwrite
returns fewer only after an error.
.SH SEE ALSO
read(2), write(2), getc(3), putc(3), ferror(3), stdio(3)
.SH HISTORY
The
.B fread
and
.B fwrite
functions first appeared in Version 7 AT&T UNIX.
.SH BUGS
A file written with fwrite holds the bytes of the 8086's numbers, low
byte first, and a program on another kind of machine may not read them
back the same.
